\section{From a Rate to Total Change}
\lessonobjective{Approximate total change from a table or graph of rates and distinguish net change from total amount traveled.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Accumulation begins with many small changes}]
When a rate varies, multiplying one rate by the entire time interval is usually wrong because the quantity is not changing uniformly. Instead, we break the interval into short pieces. On each piece the rate is approximately constant, so ``rate times width'' estimates the change contributed by that piece. Adding those contributions gives an approximation to the total change, and making the pieces finer leads to the definite integral.

Units make the construction transparent. If a flow rate is measured in liters per minute and a short interval is measured in minutes, their product is measured in liters. Each term in the sum is therefore a small amount of accumulated quantity, not another rate. This rate-times-width interpretation is the common engine behind displacement from velocity, charge from current, mass from density, and work from force.
\end{bgexposition}


If a rate is constant, total change is simply
\[
\text{rate}\times\text{time}.
\]
When the rate varies, we divide time into short intervals, treat the rate as approximately constant on each interval, multiply, and add. This is the practical origin of the definite integral.

\begin{bgconcept}[title={Small change is approximately rate times small input change}]
If \(Q'(t)\) is known, then over a short interval of width \(\Delta t\),
\[
\Delta Q\approx Q'(t^*)\Delta t.
\]
Adding these estimates over many intervals approximates the total change in \(Q\).
\end{bgconcept}

\begin{bgguided}[title={Water entering a tank}]
A tank's net inflow rate, in liters per minute, is recorded every two minutes:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
t&0&2&4&6&8\\\hline
r(t)&3&5&6&4&2
\end{array}
\]
Use left endpoints to estimate the change in volume from \(t=0\) to \(t=8\).
\begin{bgsolution}
There are four intervals, each of width \(2\). The left-endpoint rates are \(3,5,6,4\). Thus
\[
\Delta V\approx 2(3+5+6+4)=36\text{ liters}.
\]
The units confirm the calculation: liters per minute times minutes gives liters.
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-rate-total-01}{integer}{28}[Quick check]
A pump delivers water at rates \(4,5,3,2\) liters/minute over four consecutive 2-minute intervals. Use those interval rates to estimate the total delivered volume.
\begin{bghint}Multiply each rate by the common interval width and add.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[2(4+5+3+2)=28\text{ liters}.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\begin{bgadvanced}[title={Integral thinking appears before integral notation}]
The approximation \(\sum r(t_i^*)\Delta t\) is already an integral idea. The notation and limit come later. This matters pedagogically: a definite integral is not an antiderivative symbol that fell from the sky. It is the limiting total of small rate-times-width contributions.
\end{bgadvanced}

\section{The Definite Integral as Signed Area}
\lessonobjective{Interpret positive and negative contributions and distinguish net signed area from total geometric area.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={The x-axis determines the sign of contribution}]
For a graph above the \(x\)-axis, a definite integral agrees with ordinary geometric area. Below the axis, the function values are negative, so the corresponding contributions are negative. The integral therefore measures signed or net area, not total geometric area. Regions on opposite sides of the axis can cancel even though both occupy physical space on the page.

When a problem asks for total area, first find every crossing of the axis and split the interval. Either reverse the sign on below-axis pieces or integrate the absolute value. Keeping these two questions separate -- net accumulation versus total geometric area -- prevents a very common error in which a perfectly evaluated integral answers the wrong question.
\end{bgexposition}


When \(f(x)\ge0\), the definite integral equals ordinary area under the curve. When \(f(x)<0\), rectangle heights are negative, so those contributions subtract. Thus
\[
\int_a^b f(x)\,dx
=\text{area above the axis}-\text{area below the axis}.
\]

\begin{bgexample}[title={Use geometry rather than antiderivatives}]
Suppose \(f\) forms a triangle of area \(6\) above the axis on \([0,3]\) and a semicircle of area \(2\pi\) below the axis on \([3,5]\). Then
\[
\int_0^5 f(x)\,dx=6-2\pi.
\]
The total geometric area is \(6+2\pi\).
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-signed-area-01}{integer}{-2}[Quick check]
A graph encloses area 5 above the axis and area 7 below the axis. What is the definite integral?
\begin{bghint}Subtract the below-axis area from the above-axis area.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[5-7=-2.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\begin{bgexercises}
\item Sketch a function with \(\int_0^4 f(x)\,dx=0\) but \(f\not\equiv0\).
\item Explain why \(\int_a^b|f(x)|\,dx\) represents total geometric area between the graph and the axis.
\item Use symmetry to evaluate \(\int_{-3}^3x^3\,dx\).
\end{bgexercises}

\section{Average Value of a Function}
\lessonobjective{Calculate and interpret the average value of a continuous function over an interval.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={A continuous average is total contribution divided by width}]
For finitely many numbers, an average is their sum divided by how many values were added. For a continuous function, the integral replaces the sum and the interval length replaces the count. Thus the average value \(\frac{1}{b-a}\int_a^b f(x)\,dx\) is the constant height that would produce the same total accumulation over the interval.

This interpretation is often more useful than the formula alone. Average temperature over a day, average power over a time interval, and average density along a rod all use the same idea. For a continuous function, the average-value theorem guarantees that the function actually attains this average somewhere in the interval, although it may do so at more than one point and the theorem does not tell us where without further work.
\end{bgexposition}


The average of finitely many numbers is their sum divided by the count. For continuously distributed values, the integral plays the role of the sum and interval length plays the role of the count:
\[
f_{\mathrm{avg}}=\frac1{b-a}\int_a^bf(x)\,dx.
\]

\begin{bgapplication}[title={Average electrical power}]
If instantaneous power is \(P(t)=100+20\sin t\) watts on \([0,2\pi]\), then
\[
P_{\mathrm{avg}}=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}(100+20\sin t)\,dt=100\text{ W}.
\]
The oscillating part contributes zero net average over a full period.
\end{bgapplication}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-average-value-01}{integer}{4}[Quick check]
Find the average value of \(f(x)=2x\) on \([1,3]\).
\begin{bghint}Integrate, then divide by interval length 2.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[f_{avg}=\frac12[x^2]_1^3=\frac12(8)=4.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part II}
\lessonobjective{Evaluate definite integrals with antiderivatives and connect the result to net accumulated change.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Why antiderivatives evaluate exact accumulation}]
Riemann sums define the definite integral, but evaluating a limit of sums directly would be unbearable for most functions. The second part of the Fundamental Theorem provides the computational shortcut: if \(F'=f\), then \(\int_a^b f(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a)\). The endpoint difference captures all of the tiny accumulated contributions at once.

The notation \([F(x)]_a^b\) should be read as an instruction to substitute both endpoints and subtract in the correct order. The constant of integration cancels, which is why definite integrals do not need \(+C\). Even so, the theorem is not merely a formula to memorize. It depends on recognizing an antiderivative and on understanding that the result is net accumulation, with sign and units inherited from the original integrand and variable.
\end{bgexposition}


\begin{bgtheorem}[title={FTC Part II}]
If \(f\) is continuous on \([a,b]\) and \(F'(x)=f(x)\), then
\[
\int_a^bf(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a).
\]
\end{bgtheorem}

The theorem is astonishing because it turns a limit of many sums into two endpoint evaluations.

\begin{bgguided}[title={Evaluate a definite integral}]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_1^3(2x+4)\,dx
&=[x^2+4x]_1^3\\
&=(9+12)-(1+4)\\
&=16.
\end{aligned}
\]
The bracket notation means evaluate the antiderivative at the upper endpoint and subtract its value at the lower endpoint.
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-ftc2-01}{integer}{9}[Quick check]
Evaluate \(\int_0^3 2x\,dx\).
\begin{bghint}An antiderivative is \(x^2\).\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[[x^2]_0^3=9.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\begin{bgmistake}
The definite integral is not \(F(b)+F(a)\), and the lower endpoint is not optional. Write the bracket step explicitly until endpoint subtraction is automatic.
\end{bgmistake}

\section{Definite Integrals by Substitution}
\lessonobjective{Change bounds consistently or back-substitute before applying original bounds.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Change the variable and the bounds together}]
In a definite integral, substitution changes not only the integrand but also the coordinate used to describe the interval. If \(u=g(x)\), convert the original \(x\)-bounds into \(u\)-bounds immediately. Then the entire calculation can remain in \(u\), avoiding a needless back-substitution before evaluating endpoints.

Mixing an integrand in \(u\) with bounds in \(x\) is a category error, not a minor notation blemish. The bounds must describe values of the current integration variable. After evaluation, interpret the answer in the original problem's units and context; the temporary variable is a computational device, while the definite integral still represents the same accumulated quantity.
\end{bgexposition}


For definite integrals, two valid methods exist:
\begin{enumerate}
\item transform the integrand and change the bounds into \(u\)-values; or
\item find an antiderivative in \(u\), return to \(x\), and use the original bounds.
\end{enumerate}
Do not transform the integrand into \(u\) while leaving \(x\)-bounds attached.

\begin{bgexample}[title={Change the bounds}]
\[
\int_0^1 2x(x^2+1)^3\,dx.
\]
Let \(u=x^2+1\), \(du=2x\,dx\). When \(x=0\), \(u=1\); when \(x=1\), \(u=2\). Therefore
\[
\int_1^2u^3\,du=\left[\frac{u^4}4\right]_1^2=\frac{15}{4}.
\]
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-def-sub-01}{expression}{(e-1)/2}[Quick check]
Evaluate \(\int_0^1 x e^{x^2}\,dx\).
\begin{bghint}Let \(u=x^2\), so \(du=2x\,dx\), and change the bounds to 0 and 1.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[\frac12\int_0^1e^u\,du=\frac{e-1}{2}.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Trigonometric Integrals}
\lessonobjective{Use parity and identities to integrate powers and products of sine, cosine, tangent, and secant.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Parity determines the useful identity}]
Integrals involving powers of sine, cosine, secant, or tangent are solved by exposing a derivative-compatible factor. For products of sine and cosine powers, an odd power often lets us save one factor and convert the rest using \(\sin^2x+\cos^2x=1\). When both powers are even, half-angle identities usually reduce the exponents.

The same pattern governs tangent and secant. A secant-squared factor pairs with the derivative of tangent, while a secant-tangent factor pairs with the derivative of secant. The identities are not random decorations; they reorganize the integrand into a substitution pattern. Before calculating, classify the powers and state which derivative pair you are trying to create.
\end{bgexposition}


For \(\sin^m x\cos^n x\):
\begin{itemize}
\item if one exponent is odd, save one factor of that function and convert the rest using \(\sin^2x+\cos^2x=1\);
\item if both exponents are even, use power-reduction identities.
\end{itemize}
For powers of tangent and secant, preserve a \(\sec^2x\) factor for \(u=\tan x\) or a \(\sec x\tan x\) factor for \(u=\sec x\) when possible.

\begin{bgexample}[title={Odd sine power}]
\[
\int\sin^3x\cos^2x\,dx
=\int(1-\cos^2x)\cos^2x\sin x\,dx.
\]
Let \(u=\cos x\), \(du=-\sin x\,dx\):
\[
-\int(u^2-u^4)\,du=-\frac{u^3}{3}+\frac{u^5}{5}+C.
\]
Thus
\[
-\frac{\cos^3x}{3}+\frac{\cos^5x}{5}+C.
\]
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-trig-int-01}{expression}{sin(x)^3/3+C}[Quick check]
Evaluate \(\int\sin^2x\cos x\,dx\).
\begin{bghint}Let \(u=\sin x\).\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[\int u^2du=u^3/3+C.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Choosing an Integration Strategy}
\lessonobjective{Choose an efficient method, recognize when no elementary antiderivative is likely, and verify results by differentiation.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Integration is a recognition problem}]
Unlike differentiation, integration has no single rule that mechanically handles every expression. The same-looking integral may yield to simplification, substitution, integration by parts, a trigonometric identity, trigonometric substitution, or partial fractions. Strategy begins by classifying the structure of the integrand rather than applying the most recently learned technique to everything in sight.

A useful order is: simplify algebraically, check the basic table, look for a composite derivative, inspect products, classify rational functions, and then consider specialized trigonometric methods. After each transformation, ask whether the new integral is simpler. Verification by differentiation remains the final referee. An efficient integrator is not the person who remembers the most tricks, but the person who chooses the cheapest appropriate one.
\end{bgexposition}


\begin{bgdecision}[title={A practical order of attack}]
\begin{enumerate}
\item Simplify algebraically.
\item Look for a direct formula.
\item Look for substitution structure.
\item If the integrand is a product involving polynomial, logarithmic, inverse-trig, or exponential factors, consider integration by parts.
\item For trigonometric powers, use identities.
\item For radicals involving quadratic sums or differences, consider trigonometric substitution.
\item For rational functions, divide and use partial fractions.
\item If no elementary method fits, use numerical integration or retain a defined accumulation function.
\end{enumerate}
\end{bgdecision}

\begin{bgadvanced}[title={Not every elementary-looking function has an elementary antiderivative}]
Functions such as \(e^{-x^2}\), \(\sin(x^2)\), and \(1/\ln x\) are perfectly integrable on many intervals but do not have elementary antiderivatives. Integral notation can define useful functions even when algebraic antiderivative notation cannot simplify them.
\end{bgadvanced}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-strategy-01}{multiple_choice}{integration by parts}[Quick check]
Which method is the natural first choice for \(\int x\ln x\,dx\)?
\begin{bghint}The product contains a logarithm that becomes simpler when differentiated.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}Integration by parts is the natural choice.\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Midpoint and Trapezoidal Rules}
\lessonobjective{Compute numerical estimates from formulas or tables and reason from concavity about likely error direction.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Approximation is often the only honest option}]
Many definite integrals have no elementary antiderivative, and real data may be available only in a table. Numerical integration estimates the accumulated total directly from sampled values. The midpoint and trapezoidal rules both use local geometric approximations, but they model each subinterval differently: one uses a constant midpoint height, while the other uses a straight-line connection between endpoint values.

Error depends on interval width and curvature. A finer partition usually improves the estimate, but the direction of error can often be predicted from concavity. Trapezoids lie above a concave-up graph and below a concave-down graph; midpoint behavior is typically opposite. A complete numerical answer should state the rule, the partition, the units, and a sensible precision rather than presenting a long decimal as though a calculator had discovered exact truth.
\end{bgexposition}


The midpoint rule uses midpoint rectangles. The trapezoidal rule replaces each curve segment by a line segment:
\[
T_n=\frac{\Delta x}{2}\left[f(x_0)+2f(x_1)+\cdots+2f(x_{n-1})+f(x_n)\right].
\]

\begin{bgexample}[title={Trapezoidal estimate from data}]
Suppose
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
x&0&1&2&3\\\hline
f(x)&1&2&5&10
\end{array}
\]
With \(\Delta x=1\),
\[
T_3=\frac12[1+2(2)+2(5)+10]=\frac{25}{2}.
\]
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-trap-01}{integer}{4}[Quick check]
Using one trapezoid, estimate \(\int_0^2 x^2\,dx\).
\begin{bghint}Average the endpoint heights 0 and 4, then multiply by width 2.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[T_1=2(0+4)/2=4.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Simpson's Rule}
\lessonobjective{Apply Simpson’s Rule with an even number of subintervals and understand why parabolic approximation is unusually accurate.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Parabolas can approximate curvature better than lines}]
Simpson's Rule improves on straight-line approximation by fitting quadratic behavior across pairs of subintervals. This produces the characteristic weights \(1,4,2,4,\ldots,2,4,1\) and usually gives high accuracy for smooth functions. The method requires an even number of equal subintervals because each quadratic panel spans two of them.

The formula is easy to misuse if the data spacing or weight pattern is not checked first. Write the table of \(x\)-values and weights before multiplying, and verify that the endpoints receive weight one. Simpson's Rule is still an approximation, not a symbolic antiderivative. Its strength is that it captures curvature efficiently, making it useful when function evaluations are available but exact integration is not.
\end{bgexposition}


For even \(n\),
\[
S_n=\frac{\Delta x}{3}\left[f(x_0)+4f(x_1)+2f(x_2)+\cdots+4f(x_{n-1})+f(x_n)\right].
\]
The alternating \(4,2,4,2,\ldots,4\) pattern is not optional bookkeeping; it comes from fitting quadratic pieces across pairs of subintervals.

\begin{bgexample}[title={Simpson’s Rule is exact for cubics}]
For \(f(x)=x^3\) on \([0,2]\) with \(n=2\), \(\Delta x=1\):
\[
S_2=\frac13[f(0)+4f(1)+f(2)]=\frac13(0+4+8)=4.
\]
The exact integral is also \([x^4/4]_0^2=4\).
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-simpson-01}{integer}{4}[Quick check]
Use Simpson’s Rule with \(n=2\) to estimate \(\int_0^2 x^3\,dx\).
\begin{bghint}Use values at 0, 1, and 2 with weights 1, 4, 1.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[(1/3)(0+4+8)=4.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Improper Integrals with Unbounded Integrands}
\lessonobjective{Split at singularities, use one-sided limits, and require every piece to converge.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={An infinite spike may or may not have finite accumulation}]
When an integrand becomes unbounded at an endpoint or inside the interval, the ordinary definite-integral notation hides a limit. Replace the problematic boundary by a nearby finite value and approach it from the correct side. If the singularity lies inside the interval, split the integral there and require both one-sided improper integrals to converge.

A graph can look alarming without determining the answer. A very tall but sufficiently narrow spike may have finite area, while a milder-looking singularity may diverge. The decision comes from the limiting calculation. Never cancel divergences from opposite sides unless a different concept, such as a principal value, has been explicitly introduced; ordinary improper-integral convergence requires each piece to exist independently.
\end{bgexposition}


If \(f\) is unbounded at \(c\in[a,b]\), define
\[
\int_a^bf(x)\,dx
=\lim_{t\to c^-}\int_a^tf(x)\,dx
+\lim_{s\to c^+}\int_s^bf(x)\,dx,
\]
provided both one-sided integrals converge.

\begin{bgexample}[title={Integrable vertical blow-up}]
\[
\int_0^1\frac1{\sqrt{x}}\,dx
=\lim_{a\to0^+}[2\sqrt{x}]_a^1=2.
\]
The function becomes unbounded at \(0\), but the area remains finite.
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgmistake}
Never evaluate across a singularity with a single antiderivative subtraction. Split the interval first. Cancellation between two divergent sides does not make the ordinary improper integral converge.
\end{bgmistake}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3a-improper-sing-01}{multiple_choice}{converges}[Quick check]
Does \(\int_0^1x^{-1/2}\,dx\) converge?
\begin{bghint}The exponent corresponds to a p-integral near zero with \(p=1/2<1\).\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}It converges to 2.\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}
